# Shared working tree is not yours

This workspace (`c:\analog-pim`) is worked on by many concurrent agents
sharing **one git working tree** per repo. An agent that treats the tree
as private — reverting to "get a clean base", or staging everything in
sight — silently destroys or misattributes a sibling's unfinished work.
There is no warning, no conflict marker, and often no recovery path.

This rule complements
[`auto-commit-push.mdc`](auto-commit-push.mdc) (commit workflow and
hard push guardrails),
[`agent-slice-discipline.mdc`](agent-slice-discipline.mdc) (one slice,
file-path ownership), and
[`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc) §6 (FedRAMP
commit hygiene — a reviewer reading `git log -p` on a security-relevant
file must understand why each line changed).

## The rule

> **An agent may only create, modify, stage, or commit files inside its
> own slice. The working tree is shared with concurrent agents who have
> uncommitted work in it at any moment. Commit early with explicit
> per-path staging — never by `git add -A`, and never by holding a large
> batch of finished work uncommitted for a long time.**

These two observed failures pull in opposite directions. The fix for
silent destruction is "commit early". The fix for sweeping a sibling's
files into your commit is "never stage what you do not own". Both are
satisfied by the same practice: **commit early, by explicit path only**.

## Forbidden outright

These destroy sibling work with no recovery, or poison the audit trail:

- `git checkout -- .` / `git checkout -- <path you do not own>` /
  `git restore` across the tree (any form that discards uncommitted
  changes you did not author)
- `git reset --hard` in any form
- `git stash` (it removes sibling changes from the tree just as
  effectively as a revert, and the sibling has no idea where they went)
- `git clean -fd`
- `git revert` of a commit you do not own
- `git add -A` / `git add .` / `git commit -a`
- Force-push in any form, and any history rewrite (see also
  [`auto-commit-push.mdc`](auto-commit-push.mdc))
- Deleting or reverting a file to "get a clean base" for your own
  build or test run

## Required practice

- **Stage by explicit path, every time.** `git add path/to/file` only
  for paths that belong to your slice. Never let `status` decide the
  commit set.
- **Commit early and in small coherent units.** Do not hold a large
  body of finished work uncommitted — in this tree, uncommitted work is
  at risk, and observed silent loss is the reason. Prefer several small
  commits over one long-lived dirty tree.
- **Keep genuinely unfinished work unstaged** so a sibling's
  explicit-path commit cannot pick it up.
- **If you find a file staged that is not yours**, unstage that path
  (`git restore --staged <path>`) rather than committing it or deleting
  its contents. Unstaging is non-destructive; the sibling's working-tree
  changes survive.
- **If your build fails because of an error in a file you do not own**,
  report it to the parent with the exact path and error and keep working
  on what you can. Do not fix it and do not revert it.
- **If you believe the tree genuinely must be reverted**, stop and ask
  the parent first. State what you want to revert and why.

## Why the obvious workaround is wrong

Reverting to get a clean build base feels harmless because your own work
is safe and the tree looks tidy afterward. It is not harmless. Uncommitted
sibling work is invisible to you — `git status` after the revert shows a
clean tree, which looks like success and is actually the evidence of the
loss. Compile errors in files you do not own are a **routing problem**
(escalate the path and error to the parent), not a tree-state problem.

## Worked violation → fix

**Violation** (observed 2026-07-28): an agent hits 13 compile errors in
files owned by other concurrent agents, runs `git checkout -- .` to get
a clean base for its own build, and silently wipes a sibling's finished
but uncommitted edits. The sibling's next `git status` is clean; the
work is gone with no conflict and no recovery. Separately, sweeping
`git add -A` / committing whatever was already staged pulled
`agentless_common.rs`, `agentless_winrm.rs`, and later
`agentless_remote_ops` / `privilege_jobs` remote SSH/WinRM work into
unrelated documentation commits — history that still attributes
security-relevant rotation and elevation code to a docs subject line.

**Fix**:

1. Report the foreign compile errors to the parent with exact paths and
   messages; do not touch those files.
2. Stage and commit only your own completed paths by explicit
   `git add <path>` (see the temp-file commit pattern in
   [`auto-commit-push.mdc`](auto-commit-push.mdc)).
3. Leave unfinished work unstaged; continue the slice.
4. If a foreign path is already staged, `git restore --staged <path>`
   before committing — never include it, never delete its contents.

